 The BFD 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Paramount Skydance (Nasdaq: PSKY) yesterday agreed to settle with 12 state AGs, led by California's Rob Bonta, who had sued to block its $110 billion acquisition of Warner Bros. Discovery (Nasdaq: WBD). 

 Why it's the BFD: Hollywood's long-running studio saga is over.

 Zoom in: The AGs didn't get much, perhaps befitting of what always was a steep legal climb. 

 Behavioral remedies include the creation of an editorial oversight board for CBS and CNN, albeit one appointed by Paramount CEO David Ellison, a promise to distribute at least 30 films per year in theaters, and to spend at least $1.5 billion more on domestic film production over the next five years. The bottom line: "Ellison played his hand brilliantly, pressuring Bonta by threatening to pull Paramount and its thousands of jobs out of California. Now the deal's historical debt load becomes his biggest issue." — Sara Fischer, Axios 

 
 
